  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nLet us seek to minimize the evaluated objective function by optimizing a candidate sequence through a disciplined combination of algorithmic components. We will employ multidimensional search techniques, specifically coordinate descent with adaptive geometric constraints and moment-based optimization to explore diverse sequence structures.\n\nKey innovations:\n- Sequence representation: Variable-length convolved basis vectors (suggesting harmonic distortion)\n- Objective decomposition: Separating amplitude from moment dynamics while preserving the full convolution\n- Parallel exploration: Maintaining candidate individuals across length dimensions with migratory probability\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nOur candidate sequences will be built from variable-length base sequences with normalized (unit-sum) coefficients, each element bounded by [0, 1000]. The exact objective is:\n\nF(a) = 2 * n * max(convolve(a, mode='full')) / (sum(a))\u00b2\n\nwhere convolve(a, mode='full') produces a sequence of length 2*n-1.\n\nConstraints applied during optimization:\n- Non-negativity of all coefficients\n- Each coefficient \u2264 1000\n- Normalization maintains sum(a) proportional to the evaluation function\n\nWe will use maximum autocorrelation as the primary optimization target (to reduce).\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nDetailed steps for propose_candidate:\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport time\n\ndef propose_candidate(seed=42, budget_s=1800, **kwargs):\n    # Must accept np.array if provided, then convert to list\n    # Check if parent available in kwargs\n    deadline_initial = time.time() + float(budget_s)\n    convergence_threshold = 0.0001\n\n    # Determine starting length with fallback\n    parent = kwargs.get('parent')\n    if parent is not None and np.issubdtype(parent, np.floating):\n        n_start = len(parent)\n        sequence_start = parent / np.sum(parent)  # Normalization factor through scaling\n    else:\n        n_start = 100  # Small initial length\n        sequence_start = np.ones(n_start) / n_start\n\n    # Convert to list for processing\n    sequence_start = np.minimum(sequence_start, 1000).tolist()\n    \n    # Coefficients storage\n    history = []  # To store evaluated sequences\n    best_fitness = float('inf')\n    best_candidate = None\n    \n    # Initial coordinate descent for base optimization\n    def optimize_coords(a, max_iter=1000):\n        # Convert to numpy array and estimate initial length\n        a = np.array(a, dtype=float)\n        n = len(a)\n        total_evals = 0\n        \n        # Calculate original sum to normalize\n        sum_a = np.sum(a)\n        eval_time_start = time.time()\n        # First evaluation before optimization\n        orig_fitness = float(2 * n * max(np.convolve(a, a), 0) / (sum_a**2))\n        total_evals += 1\n        history.append(orig_fitness)\n        \n        # Coordinate descent optimization\n        for _ in range(max_iter):\n            if time.time() - eval_time_start > deadline_initial - np.log10(total_evals) * 2:\n                break  # Budget safety margin\n            \n            # Individual coordinate optimization\n            x_old = a[np.argmax(a)]  # Current best coordinate\n            n_old = n  # Fixed-length optimization for maximal impact\n            \n            # Snippet scoring using FFT for performance\n            b_snippet = np.convolve(a, a, mode='full')\n            max_val = np.max(b_snippet)\n            obj = float(2 * n_old * max_val / (sum_a**2))\n            \n            # Learn a geometric kernel from the objective landscape\n            # First version: quadratic moment expansion\n            if obj > best_fitness + convergence_threshold:\n                break  # Convergence protection\n                \n            q = np.convolve(a, a, mode='full', orthogonalize=True)\n            w = np.exp(-n * (q - max_val)**2 / (max_val**2 + convergence_threshold))\n            sum_weights = np.sum(w)\n            \n            # Moment-based priority inversion for column placement\n            sg = np.zeros(n)\n            for i in range(min(n//2, 500)):\n                s_index, alpha = find_geometry_extreme(q, i)\n                sg[s_index] += alpha * (w[i] / sum_weights)  # Derivative contribution\n            \n            # Wzenko's adapted projection\n            def projection(v):\n                # Renormalization\n                s = np.sum(v)\n                if s < 0.01:\n                    # Minimal energy broadcasting\n                    v = v + np.ones(len(v)) * 0.01\n                    s = 0.01\n                # Clip for fairness\n                v = np.minimum(v, 1000)\n                return (v / s * (1-0.001)).astype(float)  # Smoothing for stability\n            \n            # Bisection search for reduction potential\n            opp = projection(v)\n            while np.any(opp < convergence_threshold) and n > 5:\n                opp = opp / (1 + 0.01*len(opp))\n            opp_fitness = float(evaluate_sequence(opp.tolist()))\n            \n            # Recovery operation on candidate store\n            if opp_fitness < best_fitness - convergence_threshold:\n                # Cross-promotion between coordinate extremes\n                best_candidate = opp.copy()\n                best_fitness = opp_fitness\n                total_evals += 1\n                # Update training run array\n                # Some extra checks omitted for brevity\n            elif opp_fitness > best_fitness + convergence_threshold:\n                # Respiration from elevated coordinates\n                new_c = projection(v)\n                f_new = float(evaluate_sequence(new_c.tolist()))\n                if f_new < best_fitness:\n                    best_candidate, best_fitness = new_c, f_new\n                    total_evals += 1\n            else:\n                # Iterate until budget exhausted\n                pass\n                \n        return best_candidate.tolist(), best_fitness\n    \n    # Extended method for parent suggestions if available\n    # ...\n```\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\nSearch techniques alternate between coordinate-wise and global optimization:\n\n- Coordinate-wise geminate search: Up to 3000 iterations per dimension for stability\n- Polynomial moment matching for unconstrained optimization\n- Hierarchical structure transfer: Moving patterns between length dimensions\n- Budget-adaptive greedy tactics: For parochial territory when stuck\n\nAdaptive schedules:\n\n```python\ndef adaptive_schedule(runtime, max_run=1000):\n    # ...\n```\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Short-chunk FFT evaluation time proportional to n log\u2082(n)\n- Migratory trials capped at implementation capability\n- Safeguards against degenerative antagonist injections\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\nAll candidates must pass through evaluate_sequence with finite value and correct normalization.\n\nThe returned candidate:\n\n```python\nif best_candidate is None:\n    # Fallback for geometry emergence failures\n    benchmark = np.ones(256) / 256\n    # Forced normalization to check validity\n    best_candidate, best_fitness = optimize_coords(benchmark)\n    \nreturn best_candidate.tolist()\n````",
